Say that systemd is built without the bpf framework

systemd 261.3 looks for libbpf, and where it finds one it goes on to version
check clang and bpftool. A bpftool that is present but answers with an error -
Ubuntu installs a wrapper that does exactly that when the kernel's tools are not
there - stops the configure with "Command `/usr/sbin/bpftool --version` failed
with status 2", so the build turns on whether the machine it runs on happens to
carry working bpf tooling.

The framework is already out of the image either way: it takes libbpf, which is
not built for the target, so meson reports bpf-framework among the disabled
features on a build that succeeds. Say so, and the dependency is not looked for
at all, which is where the version checks hang off.

diff --git a/packages/sysutils/systemd/package.mk b/packages/sysutils/systemd/package.mk
--- a/packages/sysutils/systemd/package.mk
+++ b/packages/sysutils/systemd/package.mk
@@ -44,6 +44,7 @@
                        -Dzstd=disabled \
                        -Dxkbcommon=disabled \
                        -Dpcre2=disabled \
+                       -Dbpf-framework=disabled \
                        -Dglib=disabled \
                        -Ddbus=disabled \
                        -Ddefault-dnssec=no \
